HUB.add({
  id: 'testing', title: 'Testing', short: 'Testing',
  blurb: 'Unit, integration and E2E testing: Jest/Vitest, RTL, mocks, snapshots, Cypress, coverage, visual tests.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is unit testing?`,
  k: `Checking each Lego brick on its own to make sure it isn't broken.`,
  a: `Testing the smallest units (functions, hooks, components) in isolation, fast and many. Part of the testing trophy/pyramid with integration tests (several units together, where most React value lies) and E2E tests (whole app in a browser), plus static analysis (TypeScript, ESLint).` },
{ q: `What are the main libraries used for testing React applications?`,
  k: `A test runner, a toy-player, a fake shop and a robot that uses the browser.`,
  a: `Runner and assertions: <b>Vitest</b> (Vite projects) or <b>Jest</b>. Components: <b>React Testing Library</b> + <code>@testing-library/user-event</code> + <code>jest-dom</code>. Network mocking: <b>MSW</b>. E2E: <b>Playwright</b> or <b>Cypress</b>. Visual/component: Storybook with Chromatic, Playwright screenshots. Accessibility: axe.` },
{ q: `What is Jest?`,
  k: `A test teacher who runs all checks and tells you which passed.`,
  a: `A JavaScript test framework from Meta: test runner, assertions (<code>expect</code>), mocks/spies, fake timers, snapshot testing, coverage and jsdom environment. Vitest offers a nearly identical API with native ESM/TypeScript and Vite speed.` },
{ q: `How do you set up Jest for a React application?`,
  k: `Install the teacher and tell her the classroom looks like a browser.`,
  a: `Install <code>jest</code>, <code>jest-environment-jsdom</code>, a transform (<code>babel-jest</code> or <code>@swc/jest</code>), RTL and jest-dom; set <code>testEnvironment: 'jsdom'</code> and a setup file; map CSS/assets with <code>moduleNameMapper</code>. Next.js provides <code>next/jest</code>.`,
  c: `// jest.config.js
module.exports = {
  testEnvironment: 'jsdom',
  setupFilesAfterEnv: ['<rootDir>/jest.setup.js'],   // import '@testing-library/jest-dom'
  moduleNameMapper: { '\\\\.(css|less)$': 'identity-obj-proxy' },
}`,
  u: `With Vite use Vitest: npm i -D vitest jsdom @testing-library/react, set test.environment = 'jsdom'.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of a test runner?`,
  k: `The one who finds all the checks and runs them.`,
  a: `It discovers test files, executes them (often in parallel and isolated), provides globals or imports (<code>describe/it/expect</code>), environments (node/jsdom), watch mode, reporting and coverage. Examples: Jest, Vitest, Playwright Test, Mocha.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of a test suite?`,
  k: `A folder of related checks.`,
  a: `A group of related test cases (usually a <code>describe</code> block or a file) sharing setup and describing one unit or feature. Helps organisation and readable output.` },
{ q: `What is a test case?`,
  k: `One check: "when I press the button, the number goes up".`,
  a: `A single scenario with arrange, act, assert steps, written with <code>it</code>/<code>test</code>. It should test one behaviour and have a descriptive name.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between a test case and a test suite?`,
  k: `A case is one check; a suite is the folder of checks.`,
  a: `A test case verifies one behaviour; a test suite groups related cases (and can nest). Suites can share <code>beforeEach</code> setup.` },
{ q: `How do you write a basic test case in Jest?`,
  k: `Name the check, do the thing, check the result.`,
  a: `Name the behaviour with <code>test</code> (alias <code>it</code>), arrange inputs, act by calling the code, and assert with <code>expect(actual).matcher(expected)</code>. Keep one behaviour per test and a name that reads like a sentence. Jest finds files named <code>*.test.js</code> or in <code>__tests__</code>; Vitest uses the same API.`,
  c: `import { sum } from './sum'
test('adds two numbers', () => {
  expect(sum(2, 3)).toBe(5)
})` },
{ q: `What is the use of the describe function in Jest?`,
  k: `It puts related checks into one labelled folder.`,
  a: `<code>describe(name, fn)</code> groups tests, scopes hooks (<code>beforeEach</code>) to the group, and nests for structure. <code>describe.each</code> runs a block for a table of inputs.` },
{ q: `How do you perform assertions in Jest?`,
  k: `Say what you expect, and the teacher checks it.`,
  a: `<code>expect(value).matcher()</code>: <code>toBe</code> (Object.is), <code>toEqual</code> (deep), <code>toMatchObject</code>, <code>toContain</code>, <code>toThrow</code>, <code>toHaveBeenCalledWith</code>, <code>resolves/rejects</code>, and jest-dom matchers like <code>toBeInTheDocument</code>, <code>toHaveTextContent</code>, <code>toBeDisabled</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the expect function in Jest?`,
  k: `It's how you say "this should be true".`,
  a: `<code>expect(actual)</code> creates an assertion object with matchers; failures throw with a readable diff. Also <code>expect.any(Number)</code>, <code>expect.objectContaining</code>, <code>expect.assertions(n)</code> for async code.` },
{ q: `How do you mock functions in Jest?`,
  k: `Use a pretend helper that records what it was asked to do.`,
  a: `<code>jest.fn()</code> (Vitest: <code>vi.fn()</code>) creates a spy with recorded calls and configurable returns; <code>jest.spyOn(obj, 'method')</code> wraps an existing method; <code>jest.mock('module')</code> replaces a module. Reset with <code>mockReset</code>/<code>restoreAllMocks</code>.`,
  c: `const onSave = jest.fn()
render(<Form onSave={onSave} />)
await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /save/i }))
expect(onSave).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ name: 'Asha' })` },
{ q: `What is the use of the beforeEach and afterEach functions in Jest?`,
  k: `Set the table before each game and clean up after.`,
  a: `Run setup/teardown around every test in the scope (reset mocks, set fake timers, clear storage). <code>beforeAll/afterAll</code> run once per file or describe. RTL cleans up the DOM automatically after each test.` },
{ q: `How do you test asynchronous code in Jest?`,
  k: `Wait for the helper to come back before checking.`,
  a: `Return a promise or use <code>async/await</code>; <code>await expect(p).resolves/rejects</code>; fake timers (<code>jest.useFakeTimers()</code>, <code>advanceTimersByTime</code>) for timeouts; RTL <code>findBy*</code>/<code>waitFor</code> for UI updates.`,
  c: `test('loads user', async () => {
  await expect(fetchUser(1)).resolves.toMatchObject({ id: 1 })
})` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the mockImplementation function in Jest?`,
  k: `Teach the pretend helper exactly what to do when asked.`,
  a: `Sets the function body of a mock: <code>mock.mockImplementation(fn)</code>, or <code>mockImplementationOnce</code> for sequential behaviour. Shortcuts: <code>mockReturnValue</code>, <code>mockResolvedValue</code>, <code>mockRejectedValue</code>.`,
  c: `api.getToys.mockResolvedValueOnce([{ id: 1, name: 'Kite' }]).mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error('500'))` },
{ q: `How do you test React components using the React Testing Library?`,
  k: `Play with the toy the way a kid would and check what you see.`,
  a: `Render the component, find elements the way users do (role, label, text), interact with <code>user-event</code>, and assert visible results. Mock the network (MSW) rather than internals; wrap in needed providers.`,
  c: `test('increments', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup()
  render(<Counter />)
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add/i }))
  expect(screen.getByText('1')).toBeInTheDocument()
})` },
{ q: `What is the use of the render function in the React Testing Library?`,
  k: `It puts the toy on the test table.`,
  a: `<code>render(ui, { wrapper })</code> mounts the component into a container in <code>document.body</code> and returns helpers (<code>rerender</code>, <code>unmount</code>, scoped queries). Create a custom render that wraps providers (router, query client, store).` },
{ q: `How do you perform queries in the React Testing Library?`,
  k: `Find things on the table the way people do: by name, label or words.`,
  a: `Use <code>screen</code> queries in priority order: <code>getByRole</code> (with name), <code>getByLabelText</code>, <code>getByPlaceholderText</code>, <code>getByText</code>, <code>getByDisplayValue</code>, <code>getByAltText</code>, <code>getByTitle</code>, last <code>getByTestId</code>. Variants: getBy (throws), queryBy (null), findBy (async), and *AllBy for arrays.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the fireEvent function in the React Testing Library?`,
  k: `It pokes the toy with one exact poke.`,
  a: `Dispatches a single DOM event (<code>fireEvent.click(el)</code>). Lower level than <code>user-event</code>, which simulates full interactions (hover, focus, keydown, input, click). Prefer user-event; use fireEvent for events user-event can't produce (scroll, custom events).` },
{ q: `How do you test user interactions in the React Testing Library?`,
  k: `Type, click and tab like a real person.`,
  a: `<code>const user = userEvent.setup()</code>, then <code>await user.type()</code>, <code>click()</code>, <code>selectOptions()</code>, <code>keyboard('{Enter}')</code>, <code>tab()</code>, <code>upload()</code>; assert on the resulting UI.` },
{ q: `What is snapshot testing?`,
  k: `Take a photo of the toy, and next time compare with the photo.`,
  a: `Serialising output (a component tree or value) to a stored file and failing when it changes. Cheap to write but brittle and easy to rubber-stamp. Use small, focused snapshots (inline), or prefer explicit assertions and visual regression tools for UI.` },
{ q: `How do you perform snapshot testing in Jest?`,
  k: `Take the photo with toMatchSnapshot.`,
  a: `Render and call <code>expect(container).toMatchSnapshot()</code> or <code>toMatchInlineSnapshot()</code>. Update intentionally with <code>-u</code> after reviewing the diff.`,
  c: `const { container } = render(<Badge count={3} />)
expect(container.firstChild).toMatchInlineSnapshot()` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the toMatchSnapshot function in Jest?`,
  k: `It's the "compare with the photo" check.`,
  a: `Writes a snapshot on first run (in <code>__snapshots__</code>) and compares on later runs, failing on differences. Accepts property matchers for dynamic values (<code>{ id: expect.any(String) }</code>).` },
{ q: `How do you mock API calls in Jest?`,
  k: `Use a pretend shop so tests don't call the real one.`,
  a: `Best: <b>MSW</b> intercepts requests at the network level so your real fetch/Axios code runs. Alternatives: mock your service module (<code>jest.mock('./api')</code>), spy on <code>fetch</code>, or axios-mock-adapter.`,
  c: `const server = setupServer(
  http.get('/api/toys', () => HttpResponse.json([{ id: 1, name: 'Kite' }]))
)
beforeAll(() => server.listen()); afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers()); afterAll(() => server.close())` },
{ q: `What is the use of the axios-mock-adapter library?`,
  k: `A pretend post office that answers letters with made-up replies.`,
  a: `Plugs into an Axios instance and returns configured responses for matching requests (<code>mock.onGet('/toys').reply(200, data)</code>). Handy for Axios-only units; MSW is more realistic and client-agnostic.` },
{ q: `How do you test Redux actions and reducers?`,
  k: `Give the rule book an old cupboard and a note; check the new cupboard.`,
  a: `Reducers are pure: call them with state and action and assert the result. Selectors likewise. For thunks, prefer integration tests with a real store and MSW over mocking dispatch.`,
  c: `expect(cartReducer({ items: [] }, itemAdded({ id: 1 }))).toEqual({ items: [{ id: 1 }] })` },
{ q: `How do you test React components connected to Redux?`,
  k: `Give the brick a real little cupboard during the test.`,
  a: `Write a <code>renderWithProviders</code> helper that creates a fresh store per test (optionally with <code>preloadedState</code>) and wraps in <code>&lt;Provider&gt;</code>; then test through the UI.`,
  c: `function renderWithProviders(ui, { preloadedState } = {}) {
  const store = setupStore(preloadedState)
  return { store, ...render(<Provider store={store}>{ui}</Provider>) }
}` },
{ q: `What is end-to-end (E2E) testing?`,
  k: `A robot uses the whole real website from start to finish like a person.`,
  a: `Testing complete user flows in a real browser against a running app (login, checkout), catching integration problems across front end, back end and infrastructure. Slower and costlier, so cover critical paths only.` },
{ q: `What are the main tools used for E2E testing in React applications?`,
  k: `Robots called Playwright and Cypress.`,
  a: `<b>Playwright</b> (multi-browser incl. WebKit, parallel, auto-waits, trace viewer, very popular now), <b>Cypress</b> (great DX, time-travel runner, component testing), WebdriverIO, and cloud grids.` },
{ q: `What is Cypress?`,
  k: `A robot tester you can watch clicking through your site.`,
  a: `A JavaScript E2E and component testing framework running inside the browser, with automatic waiting, time-travel snapshots, network stubbing (<code>cy.intercept</code>), screenshots/videos, and a dashboard (Cypress Cloud).` },
{ q: `How do you set up Cypress for a React application?`,
  k: `Install the robot and open its control room.`,
  a: `<code>npm i -D cypress</code>, <code>npx cypress open</code> to scaffold, set <code>baseUrl</code> in <code>cypress.config.js</code>, write specs in <code>cypress/e2e</code>, run against your dev server (start-server-and-test in CI).` },
{ q: `How do you write a basic E2E test in Cypress?`,
  k: `Tell the robot where to go, what to click, and what it should see.`,
  a: `Every E2E test follows visit → interact → assert: open the page with <code>cy.visit</code>, find elements the way a user would (labels, roles, text via Testing Library for Cypress), type and click, then assert on what the user sees with <code>.should()</code>, which retries automatically until it passes or times out. Keep tests independent and seed data through the API, not the UI.`,
  c: `describe('login', () => {
  it('logs in', () => {
    cy.visit('/login')
    cy.findByLabelText(/email/i).type('asha@example.com')
    cy.findByLabelText(/password/i).type('secret{enter}')
    cy.contains('Welcome, Asha').should('be.visible')
  })
})` },
{ q: `What is the use of the cy.visit function in Cypress?`,
  k: `"Robot, open this page."`,
  a: `Loads a URL (relative to <code>baseUrl</code>) and waits for the load event. Options: <code>onBeforeLoad</code> to stub window APIs, auth headers, query strings.` },
{ q: `How do you perform assertions in Cypress?`,
  k: `Say what the robot should see.`,
  a: `Chai-style via <code>.should()</code> (retried until timeout): <code>should('be.visible')</code>, <code>'have.text'</code>, <code>'have.length', 3</code>; or <code>expect</code> inside <code>.then()</code>.` },
{ q: `How do you test form interactions in Cypress?`,
  k: `The robot fills in the form and presses send.`,
  a: `Find fields by label (Testing Library for Cypress), <code>.type()</code>, <code>.select()</code>, <code>.check()</code>, submit, then assert on UI and on the request with <code>cy.intercept</code> + <code>cy.wait('@alias')</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the cy.intercept function in Cypress?`,
  k: `It watches or fakes letters going to the shop.`,
  a: `Spies on or stubs network requests: assert payloads, wait for requests, return fixtures, simulate errors and delays.`,
  c: `cy.intercept('POST', '/api/orders', { statusCode: 201, body: { id: 9 } }).as('order')
cy.get('button[type=submit]').click()
cy.wait('@order').its('request.body').should('include', { qty: 2 })` },
{ q: `How do you mock API responses in Cypress?`,
  k: `Give the robot a pretend shop reply.`,
  a: `<code>cy.intercept(method, url, { fixture: 'toys.json' })</code> or a handler function; use fixtures for stable data; keep a few true E2E tests against a real backend.` },
{ q: `What are some best practices for testing React applications?`,
  k: `Test like a user, test what matters, and keep tests fast and honest.`,
  a: `Test behaviour, not implementation; query by role/label; mock at the network boundary (MSW); fresh state per test; avoid testing library internals; focus integration tests on critical features; a few E2E smoke tests; run in CI; keep tests deterministic (fake timers, no real network); accessibility checks.` },
{ q: `How do you handle test coverage in Jest?`,
  k: `Count how much of your code the checks touched.`,
  a: `Run <code>jest --coverage</code> (Vitest: <code>--coverage</code> with v8 or istanbul). Configure <code>collectCoverageFrom</code> and <code>coverageThreshold</code>. Coverage shows untested code, not test quality; aim for meaningful coverage of risky logic.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the jest-coverage-badges library?`,
  k: `Makes a little sticker showing your coverage percentage.`,
  a: `Generates SVG badges from Jest's <code>coverage-summary.json</code> for READMEs. Today many teams use Codecov/Coveralls badges or CI summaries instead.` },
{ q: `How do you generate test coverage reports in Jest?`,
  k: `Ask the teacher for a report card.`,
  a: `Set <code>coverageReporters: ['text', 'html', 'lcov', 'json-summary']</code>; open <code>coverage/lcov-report/index.html</code>; upload lcov to Codecov/Sonar in CI.` },
{ q: `How do you perform visual regression testing in React applications?`,
  k: `Take photos of every screen and spot any change, even one pixel.`,
  a: `Capture screenshots of components or pages and diff against baselines: Storybook + Chromatic, Percy, Playwright <code>toHaveScreenshot()</code>, Loki. Stabilise fonts, animations and data to avoid flaky diffs; review changes in PRs.` },
{ q: `What is the purpose of the Percy library?`,
  k: `A photo-checking service that shows you before and after pictures.`,
  a: `BrowserStack's visual testing platform: SDKs capture DOM snapshots in your tests, Percy renders them across browsers/widths, diffs against baselines and provides a review UI in PRs.` },
{ q: `How do you integrate Percy with a React application?`,
  k: `Add the photo helper to your tests and give it a secret key.`,
  a: `Install the SDK for your runner (<code>@percy/cli</code> + <code>@percy/cypress</code>, <code>@percy/playwright</code>, or <code>@percy/storybook</code>), call <code>cy.percySnapshot('Home')</code>, set <code>PERCY_TOKEN</code> in CI, and run <code>percy exec -- &lt;test command&gt;</code>.` },
  ],
});
